# The three-layer quality model

**Do not rely on documentation alone.** Run three cooperating layers:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Layer 3 — Process discipline │
│ Branch names, PR titles, build contract, ledgers │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Layer 2 — Human review │
│ Checklists, severity-tagged comments, ownership │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Layer 1 — Automated checks │
│ Linter, types, tests, diff ratchets, custom validators │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

| Layer | Catches | Typical tools |
| ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| **Automated** | Style, types, tests, naming, config literals, structural drift | ESLint, Biome, Ruff, `tsc`, custom CI scripts |
| **Human** | Architecture fit, business logic, security judgment, intent | PR review with tagged severity |
| **Process** | Wrong merge target, silent debt, untracked deferrals | Branch rules, PR templates, decision registries |

**Invariant:** If a rule exists in docs but no layer owns it, the rule is fiction.

**SDD sits across layers:** the build contract (see below) is the human-readable source of truth; automated checks and review both trace back to it.

